   "text": "A home Wi-Fi network carries a video stream from the internet to a laptop through a series of steps involving the router, access points, radio channels, and packets. When you start streaming a video on your laptop, the request is sent to the router, which acts as the central hub for your network. The router then forwards this request to the internet, where it is directed to the server hosting the video content.\n\nThe video content is broken down into small units called packets, which are then sent through the internet to your router. Each packet contains a portion of the video data and is routed independently through the network. As these packets travel through the internet, they may take different paths to reach your router, depending on the current network conditions and the availability of the best routes.\n\nOnce the packets arrive at your router, they are reassembled in the correct order and sent to the access point, which is often the same device as the router or a separate device connected to the router. The access point then transmits the video data to your laptop using radio waves on a specific frequency, or channel, which is part of the 2.4 GHz or 5 GHz frequency bands commonly used for Wi-Fi.\n\nWhen two devices in your home try to transmit data at the same time"
